switch (type) {
case ARM64_VEC_SVE:
    target->thread.svcr &= ~SVCR_SM_MASK;
    set_tsk_thread_flag(target, TIF_SVE);
    break;
case ARM64_VEC_SME:
    target->thread.svcr |= SVCR_SM_MASK;
    set_tsk_thread_flag(target, TIF_SME);
    break;
default:
    WARN_ON_ONCE(1);
    return -EINVAL;
}
Reason:
By removing the `if (system_supports_sme())` guard, the switch statement now executes unconditionally. For `ARM64_VEC_SVE` writes (the only possible type when `!system_supports_sme()`), `TIF_SVE` is always set, and the `svcr` register update (clearing `SVCR_SM_MASK`) is performed even when SME is absent. This update is harmless on !SME systems because `svcr` is unused for non-streaming SVE, and it ensures the task state remains consistent: SVE format data is always accompanied by the `TIF_SVE` flag. The handling of `ARM64_VEC_SME` case is still unreachable when SME is not supported, as callers only pass that type on SME-capable systems.

